Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["InfraParallelQ", "ObjectName",
 CellID->1224892054,ExpressionUUID->"8393d601-4014-44ba-afc9-60862df940ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"a5269461-77a1-437c-94d1-de7b77e0573f"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraParallelQ"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraParallelQ", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["l1", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["l2", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5631a184-03b8-498a-b411-c4f0cc3f5cdd"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "tests whether the two vertex sets are parallel: disjoint, and at constant \
distance from each other.",
 "\n"
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"b399261b-096a-4b34-a531-b92d044e16d0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Definition: ",
 StyleBox["l1", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["l2", "TI"],
 " are parallel when they are disjoint and every vertex of ",
 StyleBox["l2", "TI"],
 " is at the same distance from the set ",
 StyleBox["l1", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"b8d402f5-8740-4f0a-8f32-08d06cdc2a8e"],

Cell["\<\
Both conditions matter, and each corresponds to something classical. \
Disjointness is Euclid's Definition 23, that parallel lines do not meet. \
Constant distance is the level-set reading, which is the only thing a metric \
can say without a notion of direction.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7075f32f-bd4a-4992-a695-718ceb62c4d0"],

Cell["Two consequences that catch people out.", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"904c5c91-da6d-4250-accc-13fd9d3051a0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["A line is not parallel to itself.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " It meets itself everywhere, so disjointness fails and the answer is ",
 Cell[BoxData["False"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "df5a83b6-1cf2-4c65-ae1b-4782eb140526"],
 ". Parallelism here is not reflexive."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"4cba3e84-7358-40f1-a205-d50a10909226"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Sharing a single vertex is enough to fail.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " Two sets at otherwise constant distance return ",
 Cell[BoxData["False"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3f2b2d7c-559c-4ff1-872d-1939955cabfd"],
 " as soon as they touch."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1db50897-27d0-4cbe-9f0b-46948e154fce"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The predicate does not require its arguments to be lines. It tests two \
vertex sets, so it accepts things that are not paths at all. Concentric \
shells are the clearest case: the shell at radius 3 is parallel to the shell \
at radius 2, at constant distance 1. If you need the arguments to be maximal \
shortest paths, test that separately with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraInfiniteLineQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraInfiniteLineQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c8b62727-066a-4650-8b44-67632a2af0c6"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"158047f6-718b-471e-b65c-b4146b154733"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "That is the difference from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["RandomInfraParallel"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraParallel", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "39fcbb6f-d252-4bd1-b7d3-6b88d635126a"],
 ", and it is a real one. The finder returns only ",
 StyleBox["maximal shortest paths", "TI"],
 " at constant distance, and on a lattice it typically finds none. This \
predicate can still return ",
 Cell[BoxData["True"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9b77ea47-12a1-4d74-806c-fa29fc9ae513"],
 " for sets at constant distance. A parallel can exist as a set while no \
parallel exists as a line."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"bb44430a-8533-4da1-9781-05a494c385b3"],

Cell["Corresponding notions in the classical axiom systems:", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"0c3d0212-938a-4de6-8271-7483fb236a56"],

